<section xml:id="Section-1">
<title>Subspaces and Dimension</title>

      <p>Let <m>T: U \to V</m> be a linear transformation. Then the image <m>\text{Im}(T)</m> is the range of <m>T</m>. That is, <m>\text{Im}(T) = \{T(\vec{x}): \vec{x} \in U\}</m>.</p>

      <exercise xml:id="exercise-image-subspace">
        <p>Show that <m>\text{Im}(T)</m> is a linear subspace.</p>
      </exercise>

      <proof>
        <p><em>Step <m>1</m>.</em> I claim it contains <m>\vec{0}</m>. Argue this. What does a linear transformation always send to <m>\vec{0}</m>?</p>
        <p><fillin characters="72"/></p>

        <p><em>Step <m>2</m>.</em> I claim it is closed under addition. Let <m>\vec{x}, \vec{y} \in \text{Im}(T)</m>. Then <m>\vec{x} = T(\vec{u})</m> and <m>\vec{y} 
        = T(\vec{v})</m>. What do you apply <m>T</m> to in order to get <m>\vec{x} + \vec{y}</m>?</p>
        <p><fillin characters="72"/></p>

        <p><em>Step <m>3</m>.</em> I claim it is closed under scalar multiplication. Let <m>\lambda \in \mathbb{R}</m>. Again <m>\vec{x} = T(\vec{u})</m>. 
        What do I apply <m>T</m> to in order to get <m>\lambda \vec{x}</m>?</p>
        <p><fillin characters="72"/></p>
      </proof>

      <p>Images and kernels of linear transformations from finite dimensional vector spaces are always finite dimensional.</p>

      <exercise xml:id="exercise-subspaces-finite-dimensional">
        <introduction>
          <p>Let <m>U</m> be a finite dimensional vector space, let <m>T: U \to V</m> be a linear transformation, and let <m>W</m> be a linear subspace of <m>U</m>.</p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <p>Then <m>W</m> is finite dimensional with dimension at most dimension of <m>U</m>.</p>
        </task>
        <task>
          <p>Then <m>\text{ker}(T)</m> is finite dimensional with dimension at most dimension of <m>U</m>.</p>
        </task>
        <task>
          <p>Then <m>\text{Im}(T)</m> is finite dimensional.</p>
        </task>
      </exercise>

      <proof>
        <p>For (a), note that a basis of <m>W</m> must also be linearly independent in <m>U</m>. Why does that mean it must have size at most the size of a basis of <m>U</m>?</p>
        <p><fillin characters="72"/></p>

        <p>For (b), explain how this follows from part (a).</p>
        <p><fillin characters="72"/></p>

        <p>For (c), take a basis <m>\vec{v}_{1}, \dots, \vec{v}_{n}</m> of <m>U</m>. Show that <m>T(\vec{v}_{1}), \dots, T(\vec{v}_{n})</m> spans <m>\text{Im}(U)</m>.</p>
        <p><fillin characters="72"/></p>
      </proof>
    </section>